  "answer_text": "Here's a comprehensive breakdown of what mutual fund managers charge:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udcb0 Mutual Fund Manager Fees\n\n### Overall Range\nManagement fees, whether paid as a mutual fund expense ratio or a fee paid to a financial advisor, **typically range from 0.01% to over 2%**.\n\n---\n\n### Types of Funds & Their Fee Ranges\n\n| Fund Type | Typical Fee Range |\n|---|---|\n| Passive / Index Funds | 0.01% \u2013 0.20% |\n| Actively Managed Funds | 0.50% \u2013 1.5%+ |\n| Wrap Fee Accounts | 1% \u2013 3% |\n\n- Operating fees (also known as mutual fund **expense ratios** or advisory fees) are typically **between 0.25% and 1%** of your total investment per year.\n\n- If the fund is **actively managed** to try to beat average stock market returns, these costs are higher than for **passively managed funds** such as index funds, which aim only to mirror the returns of a benchmark stock index such as the S&P 500.\n\n- Investment managers charge **wrap fees** (consolidated fees covering management, brokerage, and advisory services) at **1% to 3%** of the assets they manage for you.\n\n---\n\n### What Do These Fees Cover?\nThese fees cover:\n- **Management fees**: The cost to pay fund managers and investment advisors.\n- **12b-1 fees**: Capped at 1%, these pay for the cost of marketing and selling the fund.\n- **Other expenses**: Including custodial, legal, accounting, transfer agent expenses, and other administrative costs.\n\n---\n\n### Active vs. Passive Fees\nPassively managed funds, such as index funds and ETFs, aim to mirror the performance of a specific stock index, involve less manual intervention, and typically have **lower fees**. Interestingly, average expense ratios for both types have generally **decreased in recent years**.\n\n---\n\n### Performance-Based Fees\nSome funds also use **performance-based fees**, where managers earn additional compensation based on the fund's performance.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f Why Fees Matter\nA fund with **high costs must perform better** than a low-cost fund to generate the same returns for you. An expense ratio of **0.75% versus 0.10%** can result in a difference of **thousands of dollars** over 30 years.\n\nSo when choosing a mutual fund, always check the **expense ratio** in the fund's prospectus, as even small differences can significantly impact your long-term returns.",
